WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.440
After September's general election, the German
conservative party was left reeling, losing

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.040
its voters to the social democrats and kingmaker
parties, the free liberals and the Greens.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.880
Now, as the conservatives seek to redefine
themselves, women in the party want better

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:21.850
representation and young people are calling
for more of a say.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:26.750
Here in the headquarters of the German conservative
party, behind the apparent calm, a tremor

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.340
can still be felt.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:35.720
"After 16 years in power, chancellor Angela
Merkel’s party recorded its worst ever results

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.890
in September's general elections."

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:44.900
And despite coming second behind the social-democrats,
the CDU walks a thin line: It needs to regain

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.690
its voters' confidence.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:51.691
Armin Laschet, the chancellor candidate, has
hinted at stepping down as party leader, after

00:00:51.691 --> 00:00:54.652
a campaign marred by faux pas and gaffes for
which he apologized.

00:00:54.652 --> 00:00:59.440
Looking ahead, younger conservatives want
to use this setback as an opportunity to renew

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:00.750
their party.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:05.400
To begin with, some want to see more gender
parity:

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.770
"We simply have too few women in the party.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:13.659
That is our job first of all, to provide more
for more women overall.

00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:18.950
And then also of course to ensure these women
are visible and become visible."

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:24.470
More women, and also more space for generational
change: even seasoned conservatives are making

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.979
way for younger parliamentarians.

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:31.909
Defense Minister Annegret Kramp-Karrenbauer
and Economy Minister Peter Altmaier recently

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:35.690
renounced their Bundestag mandates to set
the pace.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:43.979
"I think it's about achieving generational
change in the CDU, and I think Peter Altmaier

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:48.410
and Annegret Kramp-Karrenbauer have sent a
great signal to make it possible."

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:57.340
"If we go into opposition, it will of course
be very important that we renew ourselves

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:03.450
for the next federal election, and also that
generational change takes place, but I also

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:08.280
want to emphasize that it's not just about
lining up younger people – rather that we

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.120
need a good mix, both in the party and in
the parliamentary groups."

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:18.750
"Speaking about generational change, we’ve
asked younger voters what they think the conservatives

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.709
should do next."

00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:25.379
"The youth are more oriented to the Greens
than the conservatives, so you have to look

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:29.219
at their motivations, what reasons they have
to prefer this party."

00:02:29.219 --> 00:02:32.510
"I think the old-timers are still there, so
we'll have to see if they really want to give

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:33.650
up their power."

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:39.769
"It's hard to see a change taking place, or
it’s not yet quite clear that one is happening

00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:40.769
at all."

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:46.540
"With a chance of playing a part in the government
lessening by the day, there will certainly

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:53.150
be a lot of soul searching among German conservatives
if they do indeed go into opposition."

